Source material

- Federal law. Copyright protection under the Copyright Act "is not available
  for any work of the United States Government" (17 U.S.C. 105(a)), meaning a
  work that a federal officer or employee prepares as part of their official
  duties (17 U.S.C. 101). Federal regulations and guidance that federal staff
  write fall under that rule. Federal statutes, like state statutes, also fall
  under the government edicts doctrine described below.
- State statutes and regulations. Under the government edicts doctrine, judges
  and legislators cannot be the authors of the works they produce in the
  course of their official duties, because "no one can own the law" (Georgia
  v. Public.Resource.Org, Inc., No. 18-1150, U.S. Apr. 27, 2020). The US
  Copyright Office will not register a government edict that any federal,
  state, local, or territorial government issues, including "legislative
  enactments, judicial decisions, administrative rulings, public ordinances,
  or similar types of official legal materials" (Compendium of U.S. Copyright
  Office Practices, 3d ed., sec. 313.6(C)(2)).
- State agency documents. Manuals, handbooks, forms, and guidance that state
  agencies publish fall outside 17 U.S.C. 105, and the Copyright Office may
  register a state government work that is not a government edict (Compendium
  sec. 313.6(C)(2)). Some files quote such documents. Any copyright in the
  quoted text stays with its owner.
